Real-world CompletableFuture pipelines combine multiple patterns — here is the structure that covers 90% of use cases.

```java
Executor io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

CompletableFuture<OrderResult> pipeline =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchOrder(orderId), io)
        .thenCompose(order -> supplyAsync(() -> enrichOrder(order), io))
        .thenCombine(
            supplyAsync(() -> fetchInventory(orderId), io),
            (enriched, inventory) -> new OrderResult(enriched, inventory)
        )
        .exceptionally(ex -> {
            log.error("Pipeline failed", ex);
            return OrderResult.empty();
        });
```

**`supplyAsync`** starts two independent operations on the specified executor. **`thenCompose`** chains a dependent async call, flattening the nested future. **`thenCombine`** merges the enriched order with the independently-fetched inventory when both complete. **`exceptionally`** recovers from any failure anywhere in the pipeline by returning a default value.

**`allOf()`** waits for multiple independent futures without combining results — useful for fire-and-forget batches. `anyOf()` completes when the first future completes — useful for racing requests against a timeout.

```java
CompletableFuture.allOf(future1, future2, future3)
    .thenRun(() -> log.info("All completed"));
```

Always specify a custom executor for I/O-bound stages. The default common ForkJoinPool has `availableProcessors() - 1` threads — blocking I/O on these threads starves parallel streams and other CompletableFuture pipelines.
